@@ description
`shout` turns a string into an upper-case exclamation: `"hello"` → `"HELLO!"`.

```c
char *shout(const char *s);
```

The output is correct, yet the service using this function grows by a few bytes on every call until it runs out of memory. **Find and fix the leak.**

Run the starter code first and read the memory report: it tells you how many bytes leaked and where they were allocated.
@@ prelude
char *shout(const char *s);
@@ starter
#include <ctype.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

char *shout(const char *s) {
    size_t n = strlen(s);

    char *upper = malloc(n + 1);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
        upper[i] = (char)toupper((unsigned char)s[i]);
    upper[n] = '\0';

    char *result = malloc(n + 2);
    strcpy(result, upper);
    strcat(result, "!");
    return result;
}
@@ solution
#include <ctype.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

char *shout(const char *s) {
    size_t n = strlen(s);

    char *upper = malloc(n + 1);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
        upper[i] = (char)toupper((unsigned char)s[i]);
    upper[n] = '\0';

    char *result = malloc(n + 2);
    strcpy(result, upper);
    strcat(result, "!");
    free(upper);              /* the temporary is no longer needed */
    return result;
}
@@ driver
#include "lc_driver.h"

int main(void)
{
    while (lc_next_case()) {
        char *s = lc_read_str();
        char *r;
        lc_make_readonly(s);
        r = shout(s);
        lc_out_str(r);
        if (r) LC_FREE_RESULT(r, "the string returned by shout()");
        lc_end_case();
    }
    return lc_finish();
}
@@ tests
"Memory Leak"

"c99"

"Don't forget to free!"
@@ editorial
## The bug

```c
char *upper = malloc(n + 1);     // temporary
...
char *result = malloc(n + 2);    // returned to the caller
...
return result;                   // upper is never freed
```

Two blocks are allocated, but only `result` leaves the function. When `shout` returns, the local variable `upper` disappears, and with it the only pointer to its block. Nobody can ever free it again: a **leak** of `n + 1` bytes per call.

## The fix

Free the temporary once it has served its purpose:

```c
strcpy(result, upper);
strcat(result, "!");
free(upper);
return result;
```

Better still, don't create it: write the upper-case characters straight into `result`:

```c
char *shout(const char *s) {
    size_t n = strlen(s);
    char *result = malloc(n + 2);
    if (result == NULL) return NULL;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
        result[i] = (char)toupper((unsigned char)s[i]);
    result[n] = '!';
    result[n + 1] = '\0';
    return result;
}
```

### A habit that prevents leaks
For every `malloc`, decide right away who will `free` it. A temporary is freed in the same function; a result is freed by the caller, and the function's documentation says so.

@@@ problem fix-the-use-after-free
@@ meta
