@@ editorial
## The bug

```c
char buf[64];              // lives in makeLabel's stack frame
...
return buf;                // returns its address
```

`buf` is a local array: it exists only while `makeLabel` runs. After `return`, its stack space is reused by the next function call, so the caller holds a **dangling pointer** into memory that is no longer reserved for it. Printing it shows the label only if nothing overwrote it yet, and freeing it is invalid because it never came from `malloc`. MSVC warns about it: *C4172: returning address of local variable or temporary*.

## The fix

Return heap memory, sized exactly:

```c
char *makeLabel(const char *prefix, int id) {
    int len = snprintf(NULL, 0, "%s-%d", prefix, id);   // how long will it be?
    char *label = malloc((size_t)len + 1);
    if (label == NULL) return NULL;
    snprintf(label, (size_t)len + 1, "%s-%d", prefix, id);
    return label;
}
```

The fixed version also handles prefixes longer than 64 characters, which the original would silently have truncated.

### Other valid designs
- Let the **caller** provide the buffer: `int makeLabel(char *out, size_t outSize, ...)`, the way `snprintf` itself works.
- A `static char buf[64]` would not dangle, but every call overwrites the previous result and the caller must not free it. Functions like `strtok` and `asctime` work this way, and it causes its own bugs.

@@@ problem fix-the-shallow-copy
@@ meta
{
  "title": "Fix the Shallow Copy",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "topics": ["Bug hunt", "Double free", "Deep copy"],
  "kind": "bughunt",
  "starterShould": "runtime_error:double_free",
  "params": [{"name": "name", "type": "string"}, {"name": "email", "type": "string"}],
  "examples": [
    {"input": ["\"Ada Lovelace\"", "\"ada@example.com\""], "output": "[\"Ada Lovelace\",\"ada@example.com\",\"Ada Lovelace\",\"ada@example.com\"]", "explanation": "The harness creates a contact, copies it, prints both, then frees both with contactFree."}
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Run it: the double free report shows where the block was freed the first time, and where again.",
    "After *copy = *c, where do copy->name and c->name point?",
    "A copy that will be freed separately needs its own copies of the strings: strdup them."
  ],
  "constraints": ["0 <= strlen(name), strlen(email) <= 100"],
  "memory": "`contactCopy` must return a contact that can be freed with `contactFree` independently of the original."
}
@@ description
A tiny address book stores contacts like this:

```c
typedef struct Contact {
    char *name;
    char *email;
} Contact;

Contact *contactCreate(const char *name, const char *email);
Contact *contactCopy(const Contact *c);   // an independent copy
void contactFree(Contact *c);
```

The harness creates a contact, copies it, prints both, and frees both. The program crashes when freeing the second contact. **Find and fix the bug.**

This is the most common way C programs end up freeing the same memory twice: two objects that each believe they own the same block.
@@ prelude
typedef struct Contact {
    char *name;
    char *email;
} Contact;
Contact *contactCreate(const char *name, const char *email);
Contact *contactCopy(const Contact *c);
void contactFree(Contact *c);
@@ starter
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

Contact *contactCreate(const char *name, const char *email) {
    Contact *c = malloc(sizeof *c);
    c->name = strdup(name);
    c->email = strdup(email);
    return c;
}

/* Returns an independent copy that the caller frees with contactFree. */
Contact *contactCopy(const Contact *c) {
    Contact *copy = malloc(sizeof *copy);
    *copy = *c;
    return copy;
}

void contactFree(Contact *c) {
    free(c->name);
    free(c->email);
    free(c);
}
@@ solution
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

Contact *contactCreate(const char *name, const char *email) {
    Contact *c = malloc(sizeof *c);
    c->name = strdup(name);
    c->email = strdup(email);
    return c;
}

Contact *contactCopy(const Contact *c) {
    /* a deep copy: new strings, not shared pointers */
    return contactCreate(c->name, c->email);
}

void contactFree(Contact *c) {
    free(c->name);
    free(c->email);
    free(c);
}
@@ driver
#include "lc_driver.h"

int main(void)
{
    while (lc_next_case()) {
        char *name = lc_read_str(), *email = lc_read_str();
        Contact *a, *b;
        lc_make_readonly(name);
        lc_make_readonly(email);
        a = contactCreate(name, email);
        b = contactCopy(a);
        if (!a || !b) {
            lc_fail("contactCreate and contactCopy must return a contact.");
            lc_out_null();
            lc_end_case();
            continue;
        }
        if (a == b) lc_fail("contactCopy returned the original contact instead of a copy.");
        lc_out_begin();
        lc_out_str(a->name);
        lc_out_str(a->email);
        lc_out_str(b->name);
        lc_out_str(b->email);
        lc_out_end();
        contactFree(a);
        if (a != b) contactFree(b);
        lc_end_case();
    }
    return lc_finish();
}
@@ tests
"Linus"
"linus@example.org"

""
""

"Grace Hopper"
"grace@example.mil"
@@ editorial
## The bug

```c
Contact *copy = malloc(sizeof *copy);
*copy = *c;      // copies the two POINTERS, not the strings
```

Assigning a struct copies its members. The members are pointers, so `copy->name` and `c->name` now point to the **same** string. Both contacts believe they own it: `contactFree(original)` frees the strings, and `contactFree(copy)` frees them again. That is a double free, which corrupts the heap in a normal program.

## The fix: a deep copy

```c
Contact *contactCopy(const Contact *c) {
    return contactCreate(c->name, c->email);   // new strings for the copy
}
```

### Shallow vs deep copies in C
C has no copy constructors: `=` on a struct is always a shallow, member-by-member copy. That is perfect for structs holding plain values (`int`, `double`, fixed arrays), and wrong for structs that *own* heap memory through pointers. For those, write an explicit copy function, and keep the ownership rule simple: each object frees exactly what it allocated.

@@@ problem fix-the-realloc-bug
@@ meta
{
  "title": "Fix the realloc Bug",
