[30]
@@ editorial
## The bug

```c
realloc(arr, (*size + 1) * sizeof *arr);   // result thrown away
arr[*size] = value;                        // arr may point to the OLD block
```

`realloc` either grows the block in place, or allocates a new block, copies the data, **frees the old block** and returns the new address. The starter ignores the return value, so after a move it writes into freed memory and returns a dangling pointer. On top of that, the new block is leaked.

## The fix

```c
int *appendValue(int *arr, int *size, int value) {
    int *bigger = realloc(arr, (*size + 1) * sizeof *arr);
    if (bigger == NULL) return arr;   // the old block is still valid
    bigger[*size] = value;
    (*size)++;
    return bigger;
}
```

### Why not `arr = realloc(arr, ...)`?
If `realloc` fails it returns `NULL` *and leaves the old block alone*. Writing `arr = realloc(arr, n)` overwrites your only pointer to that block with `NULL`, which leaks it. Store the result in a temporary first.

### Performance note
Growing by one element per call copies the whole array every time: n appends cost O(n²). The *Dynamic Array* problem shows how geometric growth fixes that.
